WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.233
Human error is a normal feature of complex work.

00:00:03.233 --> 00:00:07.758
Even skilled, careful people miss steps, misread signals, choose based on stale assumptions,

00:00:07.758 --> 00:00:09.847
or act too quickly under pressure.

00:00:09.847 --> 00:00:13.328
A strong performance system does not pretend errors will disappear.

00:00:13.328 --> 00:00:17.157
It designs work so mistakes are less likely, easier to catch,

00:00:17.157 --> 00:00:19.594
and less likely to become serious failures.

00:00:19.594 --> 00:00:22.379
Checklists and standard operating procedures support repeatable execution.

00:00:22.379 --> 00:00:25.164
They do not replace expertise; they protect it.

00:00:25.164 --> 00:00:29.341
A good checklist helps ensure that known critical steps are not missed

00:00:29.341 --> 00:00:32.126
when attention is limited or pressure is high.

00:00:32.126 --> 00:00:37.696
Procedures also make expectations visible so teams can improve the process instead of relying on memory.

00:00:37.696 --> 00:00:40.829
Pre-mortems, decision journals, and peer review improve decision quality.

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:44.310
A pre-mortem asks what could go wrong before action begins.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.746
A decision journal captures the reasoning, assumptions,

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:49.879
and expected outcomes at the time of the decision.

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:54.057
Peer review adds another perspective that may catch blind spots, unclear logic,

00:00:54.057 --> 00:00:55.101
or overlooked risks.

00:00:55.101 --> 00:00:56.842
High-risk actions deserve deliberate friction.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:00.323
Slow down when the cost of being wrong is high.

00:01:00.323 --> 00:01:01.715
Separate planning from execution.

00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:03.456
Confirm scope before irreversible steps.

00:01:03.456 --> 00:01:05.196
Use two-person review where appropriate.

00:01:05.196 --> 00:01:07.981
Design systems that catch mistakes before they matter.

00:01:07.981 --> 00:01:11.810
Reliability comes from respecting human limits and building controls around them.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:17.380
Error management works best when the team can discuss mistakes without turning every review into blame.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.861
The useful question is not only who made the error,

00:01:20.861 --> 00:01:25.386
but what made the error likely, hard to detect, or easy to amplify.

00:01:25.386 --> 00:01:30.260
That shift helps teams improve the system while still preserving accountability for careful work.
